WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.979
Permission enforcement is a core requirement for private RAG.

2
00:00:03.979 --> 00:00:12.387
The system must not allow users to retrieve documents or chunks they could not access through the approved source system or workflow.

3
00:00:12.387 --> 00:00:20.345
This requirement applies even when the answer is summarized, transformed, or returned through a conversational interface.

4
00:00:20.345 --> 00:00:24.324
Enforcement can happen at ingestion time, query time, or both.

5
00:00:24.324 --> 00:00:33.108
Ingestion-time separation may reduce risk by placing different tenants, classifications, or departments into different collections.

6
00:00:33.108 --> 00:00:44.219
Query-time enforcement is often essential because user access changes, documents move, groups are updated, projects close, and workflow-specific restrictions may apply.

7
00:00:44.219 --> 00:00:57.357
A strong design connects user identity, group membership, service accounts, application roles, tenant IDs, document permissions, metadata filters, row-level or document-level rules, and audit trails.

8
00:00:57.357 --> 00:01:06.666
The retrieval service should know who is asking, what workflow they are using, and what content is eligible before selecting context for the model.

9
00:01:06.666 --> 00:01:10.120
Administrative access deserves separate treatment.

10
00:01:10.120 --> 00:01:18.753
People who manage indexes, prompts, connectors, logs, or source mappings may have visibility and influence that ordinary users do not.

11
00:01:18.753 --> 00:01:22.207
Admin roles should be narrow, reviewable, and logged.

12
00:01:22.207 --> 00:01:28.438
Private RAG should favor least privilege and deny-by-default behavior for restricted material.

13
00:01:28.438 --> 00:01:35.044
Do not rely only on separate user interfaces or broad shared indexes when sensitive content is involved.

14
00:01:35.044 --> 00:01:42.477
Test permission boundaries before launch, after source changes, and whenever the retrieval architecture changes.

15
00:01:42.477 --> 00:01:46.531
Access tests should use realistic roles and document examples.

16
00:01:46.531 --> 00:01:58.092
Include users with partial access, users from other tenants, users whose access was recently removed, and service identities that support background jobs or evaluation workflows.
